% Introduction
% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:mc-intro}

Model checking is an automated technique for verifying that a finite-state
system satisfies a temporal property.  Unlike deductive verification, which
requires user-provided invariants and proof effort, model checking is
\emph{fully automatic}: given a model (typically a state transition system) and
a property (expressed in a temporal logic), the model checker either confirms
that the property holds or produces a \emph{counterexample trace} demonstrating
the violation.

The technique was introduced by Clarke and Emerson~\cite{clarke1981design} and
Quielle and Sifakis~\cite{quielle1981model} in 1981, earning its creators the
2007 Turing Award.  Model checking has been spectacularly successful in hardware
verification, where it is now a standard part of the chip design process.
Applications to software verification---while more challenging due to the
potentially infinite state space of software---have also yielded significant
results, particularly through bounded model checking and abstraction techniques.

In this chapter, we develop the theory and practice of model checking.
We begin with finite-state model checking (\cref{sec:mc-finite}), introduce
temporal logics (\cref{sec:mc-temporal}), examine symbolic model checking with
BDDs (\cref{sec:mc-symbolic}), discuss bounded and abstract model checking
(\cref{sec:mc-bounded}, \cref{sec:mc-abstract}), and conclude with applications
to software verification (\cref{sec:mc-software}).

% ============================================================
% Finite-State Model Checking
% ============================================================
\section{Finite-State Model Checking}\label{sec:mc-finite}

\subsection{Transition Systems}\label{sec:mc-transitions}

A \emph{transition system} is the standard model for the systems verified by
model checking.

\begin{definition}[Transition System]\label{def:transition-system}
A \emph{transition system} (or \emph{Kripke structure}) is a tuple
$M = (S, S_0, R, L)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $S$ is a finite set of states.
\item $S_0 \subseteq S$ is the set of initial states.
\item $R \subseteq S \times S$ is a total transition relation (every state has
      at least one successor).
\item $L : S \to 2^{AP}$ is a labeling function assigning to each state the set
      of atomic propositions true in that state.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

A \emph{path} in $M$ is an infinite sequence of states $\pi = s_0, s_1, s_2, \ldots$
such that $(s_i, s_{i+1}) \in R$ for all $i \geq 0$.

\subsection{The Model Checking Problem}\label{sec:mc-problem}

\begin{definition}[Model Checking Problem]\label{def:mc-problem}
Given a transition system $M$ and a temporal logic formula $\varphi$, the
\emph{model checking problem} is to decide whether $M \models \varphi$, i.e.,
whether $\varphi$ holds in all initial states of $M$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Complexity of Model Checking]\label{thm:mc-complexity}
The model checking problem for CTL (see \cref{sec:mc-ctl}) is polynomial in the
size of the model and the formula: $O(|M| \cdot |\varphi|)$.  For LTL
(\cref{sec:mc-ltl}), the problem is PSPACE-complete in the size of the formula
but linear in the size of the model.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{The Basic Algorithm}\label{sec:mc-basic-alg}

The model checking algorithm for CTL works by recursively labeling each state
with the sub-formulae that hold in it.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{CTL Model Checking}\label{alg:ctl-mc}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Kripke structure $M = (S, S_0, R, L)$, CTL formula $\varphi$
\Ensure Set of states satisfying $\varphi$
\Function{Sat}{$\varphi$}
  \Switch{$\varphi$}
    \Case{$p$ (atomic proposition)}
      \State \Return $\{s \in S \mid p \in L(s)\}$
    \EndCase
    \Case{$\neg \psi$}
      \State \Return $S \setminus \Call{Sat}{\psi}$
    \EndCase
    \Case{$\psi_1 \land \psi_2$}
      \State \Return $\Call{Sat}{\psi_1} \cap \Call{Sat}{\psi_2}$
    \EndCase
    \Case{$\mathbf{EX}\ \psi$}
      \State \Return $\{s \in S \mid \exists s'.\; (s,s') \in R \land s' \in \Call{Sat}{\psi}\}$
    \EndCase
    \Case{$\mathbf{E}[\psi_1\ \mathbf{U}\ \psi_2]$}
      \State \Return \Call{ComputeEU}{$\psi_1, \psi_2$}
    \EndCase
    \Case{$\mathbf{EG}\ \psi$}
      \State \Return \Call{ComputeEG}{$\psi$}
    \EndCase
  \EndSwitch
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The $\mathbf{EU}$ (Exists Until) and $\mathbf{EG}$ (Exists Globally) operators
require fixed-point computations:
\begin{align*}
  \mathbf{E}[\psi_1\ \mathbf{U}\ \psi_2] &= \mu Z.\; \Sat{\psi_2} \cup (\Sat{\psi_1} \cap \mathbf{EX}\ Z) \\
  \mathbf{EG}\ \psi &= \nu Z.\; \Sat{\psi} \cap \mathbf{EX}\ Z
\end{align*}
where $\mu$ and $\nu$ denote the least and greatest fixed points, respectively.

