\section{Symbolic Model Checking (BDDs)}\label{sec:mc-symbolic}

\subsection{The State Explosion Problem}\label{sec:mc-explosion}

The fundamental limitation of explicit-state model checking is the \emph{state
explosion problem}: the number of states grows exponentially with the number of
state variables.  A system with $n$ boolean variables has $2^n$ states; a system
with $n$ variables of $k$ values each has $k^n$ states.

\subsection{Binary Decision Diagrams}\label{sec:mc-bdd}

\emph{Binary Decision Diagrams} (BDDs)~\cite{bryant1986graph} provide a compact
representation of Boolean functions, enabling model checking without explicitly
constructing the state space.

\begin{definition}[BDD]\label{def:bdd}
A \emph{Binary Decision Diagram} is a directed acyclic graph representing a
Boolean function.  Each internal node is labeled with a variable and has two
successors (the \emph{then} and \emph{else} edges).  Each leaf is labeled 0 or 1.
A \emph{reduced ordered BDD} (ROBDD) is a BDD where:
\begin{itemize}
\item No two distinct nodes represent the same sub-function.
\item No node has identical then and else successors.
\item Variables appear in a fixed order along all paths.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{insight}
BDDs can represent sets of states \emph{symbolically}: a set of states is
represented by a Boolean function that is true exactly for the encodings of
states in the set.  Operations on state sets (union, intersection, complement)
become Boolean operations on BDDs.  The key question is whether the BDDs remain
small---which depends critically on the variable ordering.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Symbolic CTL Model Checking}\label{sec:mc-symbolic-ctl}

In symbolic model checking~\cite{mcmmillan1992symbolic}, sets of states are
represented as BDDs, and the model checking algorithm operates on these
representations directly:

\begin{itemize}
\item A set of states $S' \subseteq S$ is represented by its characteristic
      function $\chi_{S'} : \{0,1\}^n \to \{0,1\}$.
\item The transition relation $R$ is represented as a Boolean function
      $\chi_R(s, s')$.
\item The image operation $\mathbf{EX}\ S' = \{s \mid \exists s'.\; R(s,s') \land S'(s')\}$
      is computed as:
      \[
      \chi_{\mathbf{EX}\ S'}(s) = \exists s'.\; \chi_R(s, s') \land \chi_{S'}(s')
      \]
      using Boolean quantification on BDDs.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Variable Ordering}\label{sec:mc-ordering}

The size of a BDD is highly sensitive to the variable ordering.  Finding the
optimal ordering is NP-hard, but good heuristics exist:
\begin{itemize}
\item Keep variables that are closely related (e.g., bits of the same variable)
      adjacent in the ordering.
\item Use dynamic variable reordering during the computation.
\end{itemize}

