\section{Bounded Model Checking}\label{sec:mc-bounded}

\subsection{The BMC Approach}\label{sec:mc-bmc-approach}

\emph{Bounded model checking} (BMC)~\cite{biere1999symbolic} checks whether a
property holds for all paths of length at most $k$.  The key insight is that
bounded model checking can be reduced to a SAT problem, enabling the use of
highly optimized SAT solvers.

\begin{definition}[Bounded Model Checking]\label{def:bmc}
Given a transition system $M$, a property $\varphi$, and a bound $k$, bounded
model checking decides whether $M, s_0 \models_k \varphi$, i.e., whether
$\varphi$ holds for all paths from $s_0$ of length at most $k$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Encoding as SAT}\label{sec:mc-bmc-sat}

The BMC encoding unrolls the transition relation $k$ times:
\begin{align*}
  \Phi_k &= I(s_0) \land \bigwedge_{i=0}^{k-1} R(s_i, s_{i+1}) \land \neg\varphi(s_0, \ldots, s_k)
\end{align*}
where $I$ is the initial state predicate and $\neg\varphi$ is the negation of
the property.  If $\Phi_k$ is satisfiable, a counterexample of length at most
$k$ exists.  If $\Phi_k$ is unsatisfiable, no counterexample of length $\leq k$
exists.

\subsection{Completeness}\label{sec:mc-bmc-completeness}

BMC is \emph{incomplete} for liveness properties: if no counterexample of length
$\leq k$ exists, it does not follow that no counterexample exists at all.
However, for safety properties ($\mathbf{G}\ \neg p$), BMC is complete if $k$
exceeds the \emph{diameter} of the state space.

\begin{theorem}[BMC Completeness for Safety]\label{thm:bmc-complete}
For a safety property $\mathbf{G}\ \neg p$ on a transition system with diameter
$d$, BMC with $k \geq d$ is complete: if no counterexample of length $\leq d$
exists, the property holds.
\end{theorem}

In practice, the diameter may be exponential in the number of state variables,
so BMC is typically used as a \emph{bug-finding} technique: it can find bugs
quickly (by checking small bounds) but cannot prove the absence of bugs in
general.

