% Model Checking for Software
% ============================================================
\section{Model Checking for Software}\label{sec:mc-software}

\subsection{Challenges}\label{sec:mc-sw-challenges}

Applying model checking to software presents unique challenges:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Infinite state space:} Programs with unbounded integers, dynamic
      allocation, or recursion have infinitely many states.
\item \textbf{Complex data structures:} Pointers, arrays, and objects create
      complex state spaces.
\item \textbf{Rich semantics:} C-style languages have complex semantics
      (undefined behavior, type punning, etc.).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{SPIN}\label{sec:mc-spin}

SPIN~\cite{holzmann1997spin} is the most widely used model checker for software.
It verifies Promela models of concurrent systems, using explicit-state search
with partial-order reduction.  SPIN has been used to verify communication
protocols, operating system components, and concurrent algorithms.

\subsection{CBMC}\label{sec:mc-cbmc}

CBMC~\cite{clarke2004bounded} is a bounded model checker for C programs.  It
unrolls loops and function calls up to a given bound, encodes the resulting
program as a SAT formula, and uses a SAT solver to check for assertion
violations.  CBMC can handle complex C features including pointers, dynamic
memory allocation, and floating-point arithmetic.

\subsection{Java PathFinder}\label{sec:mc-jpf}

Java PathFinder (JPF)~\cite{havelund2000model} is an explicit-state model
checker for Java bytecode.  It executes Java programs symbolically, exploring
all possible thread interleavings and non-deterministic choices.  JPF has been
used to find bugs in NASA flight software and other safety-critical systems.

\subsection{TLA+}\label{sec:mc-tla}

TLA+~\cite{lamport2002specifying} is a specification language based on temporal
logic of actions.  The TLC model checker verifies TLA+ specifications by
exhaustive state-space exploration.  TLA+ has been used at Amazon Web Services,
Microsoft, and Intel to find subtle bugs in distributed protocols and storage
systems.

\begin{historical}
Model checking was invented in 1981 by Clarke and Emerson and independently by
Quielle and Sifakis.  The first model checkers could handle systems with at most
a few thousand states.  The development of symbolic model checking using BDDs by
McMillan~\cite{mcmmillan1992symbolic} in 1992 extended the reach to systems with
$10^{20}$ or more states.  The introduction of SAT-based bounded model checking
by Biere et al.~\cite{biere1999symbolic} in 1999 opened the door to software
verification.  Today, model checking is a standard technique in hardware design
and is increasingly used in software engineering.
\end{historical}

% ============================================================
% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:mc-summary}

Model checking provides fully automatic verification of finite-state systems
against temporal properties.  Its key strength is the ability to produce
counterexamples, making it invaluable for debugging.  Its key limitation is the
state explosion problem, which limits its applicability to large systems.

Active research areas include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{SMT-based model checking:} Using SMT solvers to handle infinite-state
      systems and complex theories.
\item \textbf{Compositional model checking:} Verifying system components
      independently and composing the results.
\item \textbf{Integration with deductive verification:} Combining model checking
      (for automated analysis) with theorem proving (for full verification).
\item \textbf{Continuous model checking:} Integrating model checking into the
      development cycle for ongoing verification.
\end{itemize}

The textbook by Baier and Katoen~\cite{baier2008principles} is the standard
reference, while Clarke et al.~\cite{clarke1999model} provide a comprehensive
treatment of the algorithmic aspects.
